AWS-009 — Workflow Governance

Document ID: AWS-009
Title: Workflow Governance
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

1. Purpose

This specification defines the Canonical Workflow Governance Model (CWGM) for the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework (AECF).

The Canonical Workflow Governance Model establishes the authorities, responsibilities, governance processes, accountability mechanisms, and oversight structures governing Workflow Definitions, Workflow Instances, Workflow Programs, and Workflow Participants throughout their lifecycle.

2. Scope

This specification defines:

Governance architecture
Workflow ownership
Stewardship
Execution authority
Governance boards
Delegation
Change governance
Operational accountability

This specification applies to every Canonical Workflow Object.

3. Canonical Definition

Workflow Governance is the governed system of authorities, responsibilities, controls, and oversight ensuring that workflows are created, approved, executed, monitored, evolved, and retired according to Atlas governance principles.

Workflow Governance governs workflow behavior.

It SHALL NOT alter workflow execution semantics.

4. Objectives

The Workflow Governance Model SHALL:

Establish workflow accountability.
Preserve execution integrity.
Enable controlled workflow evolution.
Support operational governance.
Preserve trust.
Enable federation.
Maintain auditability.
Ensure execution quality.
5. Governance Principles

Every workflow governance implementation SHALL satisfy the following principles.

Accountability

Every Workflow Definition, Workflow Instance, and Workflow Program SHALL have an accountable owner.

Separation of Duties

Workflow authoring, approval, execution oversight, and auditing SHOULD be performed by distinct governance roles whenever organizationally feasible.

Operational Transparency

Workflow governance decisions SHALL be documented and explainable.

Delegated Authority

Governance authority MAY be delegated.

Delegation SHALL remain fully traceable.

Continuous Oversight

Active Workflow Programs SHALL remain under governance supervision throughout execution.

Auditability

Governance activities SHALL generate immutable audit evidence.

6. Governance Roles

Atlas defines the following canonical workflow governance roles.

Role	Responsibility
Workflow Owner	Business accountability
Workflow Steward	Operational management
Workflow Author	Defines workflow
Workflow Reviewer	Technical review
Workflow Approver	Governance approval
Workflow Operator	Oversees execution
Workflow Coordinator	Coordinates multiple workflows
Governance Board	Strategic governance
Domain Authority	Domain oversight
Auditor	Independent verification

Organizations MAY extend these roles while preserving canonical semantics.

7. Governance Authorities

Atlas recognizes the following authorities.

Authority	Scope
Atlas Constitutional Authority	Constitutional governance
Organization Authority	Enterprise governance
Domain Authority	Engineering discipline
Project Authority	Project execution
Regulatory Authority	External governance

Authorities SHALL explicitly define jurisdiction.

8. Governance Decisions

Workflow governance SHALL authorize decisions including:

Workflow creation
Workflow approval
Workflow publication
Workflow activation
Workflow execution
Workflow suspension
Workflow cancellation
Workflow retirement
Emergency intervention

Every decision SHALL generate a governed Decision Record.

9. Delegation Model

Delegation SHALL specify:

Delegating authority
Receiving authority
Delegated responsibilities
Effective period
Operational constraints
Revocation conditions

Delegation SHALL NOT transfer constitutional accountability unless explicitly authorized.

10. Change Governance

Workflow changes SHALL require:

Change request
Impact assessment
Technical review
Governance review
Approval
Publication authorization

Changes to active Workflow Definitions SHALL create new workflow versions.

11. Execution Governance

Every Workflow Instance SHALL define:

Execution authority
Responsible operator
Assigned participants
Applicable policies
Escalation authority
Recovery authority

Execution governance SHALL remain active until workflow completion or termination.

12. AI Governance

AI participants SHALL define:

AI identity
Assigned responsibilities
Decision authority
Human oversight requirements
Confidence reporting
Escalation thresholds
Audit obligations

AI SHALL operate within the authority granted by applicable policies.

13. Governance Lifecycle

Workflow governance activities SHALL progress through:

Requested
     │
Assigned
     │
Reviewed
     │
Approved
     │
Operational
     │
Monitored
     │
Reassessed
     │
Closed

Lifecycle history SHALL remain immutable.

14. Governance Evidence

Every governance activity SHALL preserve:

Decision records
Participant assignments
Workflow versions
Approval records
Review comments
Execution authorizations
Delegation records
Audit references

Evidence SHALL remain immutable.

15. Governance Quality

Workflow governance SHALL satisfy:

Accountability
Transparency
Correctness
Traceability
Explainability
Auditability
Operational integrity
Governance compliance

Quality SHALL be continuously monitored.

16. Conformance

A Workflow Governance implementation conforms if it:

Assigns canonical governance roles.
Defines governance authorities.
Supports delegated authority.
Preserves governance evidence.
Governs execution.
Maintains auditability.
Preserves accountability.
17. Future Evolution

Future revisions MAY introduce:

AI-assisted governance recommendations.
Dynamic workflow governance.
Autonomous governance agents.
Federated execution governance.
Risk-adaptive oversight.
Continuous governance analytics.

Extensions SHALL preserve canonical governance semantics.

